diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 6af9ddb4..39e12542 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -360,7 +360,7 @@ Stored responses and compact depend on the selected Provider. The signed-in admi
 
 Media multipart requests are limited to 32 MiB total, 64 parts, and 20 MiB per file, subject to any lower server body limit. JPEG, PNG, static WebP, and GIF files require matching declared MIME types (or `application/octet-stream`), valid dimensions, and successful full decoding before any job or upstream upload. Each dimension is limited to 8192 and decoded pixels to 16,777,216; GIFs are additionally limited to 128 frames and the same cumulative pixel budget. WebP uses the official `golang.org/x/image/webp` decoder; animated WebP and invalid image data are rejected. Existing JSON data URL handling is unchanged. Filenames do not determine media type. Invalid numbers, duplicate scalar fields, and unsupported multipart options are rejected.
 
-`POST /v1/videos` returns an OpenAI-style video object with `id`, `object: "video"`, `model`, and `status`. The existing `/v1/videos/generations`, `/edits`, and `/extensions` creation paths retain their `{"request_id":"..."}` contract. Shared `GET /v1/videos/{id}` returns `queued`, `in_progress`, `completed`, or `failed`, plus real task timestamps, progress, duration as a `seconds` string when known, and `size` when the stored aspect ratio/resolution can be expressed exactly in pixels. Completed jobs retain `video.url` and expose `content_url`; authenticated video bytes are available at `/v1/videos/{id}/content`. Media support is limited to the options documented above, not full OpenAI media compatibility. There are no video list, delete, or cancel endpoints.
+`POST /v1/videos` returns an OpenAI-style video object with `id`, `object: "video"`, `model`, and `status`. The existing `/v1/videos/generations`, `/edits`, and `/extensions` creation paths retain their `{"request_id":"..."}` contract. Shared `GET /v1/videos/{id}` returns `queued`, `in_progress`, `completed`, or `failed`, plus real task timestamps, progress, duration as a `seconds` string when known, and `size` when the stored aspect ratio/resolution can be expressed exactly in pixels. Completed jobs retain `video.url` and expose `content_url`. Returned image and video links, including `/v1/media/images/{asset_id}`, `/v1/media/videos/{asset_id}`, and `/v1/videos/{id}/content`, support direct GET/HEAD downloads without an API key or cookie. Local video content supports byte ranges. Generation, task metadata, and management endpoints require authentication. Media support is limited to the options documented above, not full OpenAI media compatibility. There are no video list, delete, or cancel endpoints.
 
 `/v1/audio/transcriptions` supports `json` (default), `verbose_json`, and `text`. Video edits resolve to Console `grok-imagine-video`. Video extensions also support Web `grok-imagine-video-1.5`: provide `video_extension_start_time` with the source video's absolute end timestamp; the gateway uploads the source video into the selected Web account before starting a new extension conversation. Web extension `duration` is the added segment length from 6 to 10 seconds. Custom public model names remain supported. Monetary billing is applied only when the gateway can reliably measure the official pricing unit: TTS is reserved and settled from its input character count, while REST and streaming STT are settled from the actual audio duration returned by a successful response. Because STT duration is known only after completion, concurrent requests may briefly take a billing-limited key beyond its spend limit. Realtime, video edits/extensions, and custom routes without a recognized official price are currently audited as unpriced; they remain callable and do not consume the spend limit.
 
